  "text": "The prevalence of depression risk in our university students was higher than in most recently published Bangladeshi studies. Although these studies used different scales, including the WHO-5 Well-Being Index (WHO-5) [16], the 9-item Patient Health Questionnaire (PHQ-9) [9, 17], and the Depression, Anxiety, and Stress Scale (DASS-42) [18], they reported prevalences of 42% to 52%, higher among female than male students [9, 16–18]. However, a study of first year university students reported a higher prevalence among male (50.4%) than female (49.6%) [1] inconsistent with our findings. Another DASS-21-based study among public university students reported a non-significant higher prevalence among students staying at hall/mess [19]. This difference in prevalence by accommodation status may reflect Hossain, Alam and Masum’s inclusion of only public university students, whereas we included both public and private universities."
